{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LLwVRzZ1H2gbBx9B3wnmyX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Problem: stable sets of majority division on a grid\n2\t\n3\t## Background\n4\t\n5\tThree players divide one unit of money. Any two of them can impose a division. Von Neumann and Morgenstern's stable\n6\tsets are a classical solution concept for this game. On the continuum they found the symmetric solution\n7\t{(1/2, 1/2, 0), (1/2, 0, 1/2), (0, 1/2, 1/2)} and also infinitely many \"discriminatory\" solutions. Here the money comes\n8\tin indivisible units.\n9\t\n10\t## Definitions\n11\t\n12\tFix an integer K ≥ 2.\n13\t- An **allocation** is a triple x = (x_1, x_2, x_3) of nonnegative integers with x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = K (shares in units\n14\t  of 1/K).\n15\t- Allocation x **dominates** y if there are two distinct players i, j with x_i > y_i and x_j > y_j (both strictly better\n16\t  off, so they can impose x).\n17\t- A set V of allocations is **stable** if:\n18\t  - it is internally stable: no element of V dominates another element of V;\n19\t  - it is externally stable: every allocation not in V is dominated by some element of V.\n20\t\n21\t## Questions\n22\t\n23\t1. For which K does a stable set exist?\n24\t2. For each such K, find all stable sets.\n25\t3. Prove your answers for every K ≥ 2.\n26\t\n27\t## Checker\n28\t\n29\t`/work/check.py` tests a proposed set: `python3 check.py K \"a,b,c a,b,c ...\"`. It reports internal and external\n30\tstability, with a witness when either fails.\n31\t\n32\t## Success and stopping\n33\t\n34\tYou have succeeded, and should stop, when all of the following hold:\n35\t1. `/work/answer.md` states the exact answer to questions 1 and 2 for every K ≥ 2.\n36\t2. `/work/answer.md` contains complete proofs, covering both existence (or non-existence) and the completeness of your\n37\t   list of stable sets for every K.\n38\t3. You have written the file `/work/SUBMIT` (any content). Writing `SUBMIT` ends the attempt immediately for everyone\n39\t   working on it, so write it only when 1 and 2 are done.\n40\t\n41\tIf time runs out before you submit, whatever is in `answer.md` at that moment is graded.\n42\t\n43\t## Deliverable\n44\t\n45\t`/work/answer.md` as described above, with any code you used in `/work`. Mark partial results clearly.\n46\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d2914c6-fd56-4ed8-8c89-a511cff67019","uuid":"e8b921a2-536e-4338-b7e5-2d873276081e","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:00:18.390Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/work/problem.md","content":"# Problem: stable sets of majority division on a grid\n\n## Background\n\nThree players divide one unit of money. Any two of them can impose a division. Von Neumann and Morgenstern's stable\nsets are a classical solution concept for this game. On the continuum they found the symmetric solution\n{(1/2, 1/2, 0), (1/2, 0, 1/2), (0, 1/2, 1/2)} and also infinitely many \"discriminatory\" solutions. Here the money comes\nin indivisible units.\n\n## Definitions\n\nFix an integer K ≥ 2.\n- An **allocation** is a triple x = (x_1, x_2, x_3) of nonnegative integers with x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = K (shares in units\n  of 1/K).\n- Allocation x **dominates** y if there are two distinct players i, j with x_i > y_i and x_j > y_j (both strictly better\n  off, so they can impose x).\n- A set V of allocations is **stable** if:\n  - it is internally stable: no element of V dominates another element of V;\n  - it is externally stable: every allocation not in V is dominated by some element of V.\n\n## Questions\n\n1. For which K does a stable set exist?\n2. For each such K, find all stable sets.\n3. Prove your answers for every K ≥ 2.\n\n## Checker\n\n`/work/check.py` tests a proposed set: `python3 check.py K \"a,b,c a,b,c ...\"`. It reports internal and external\nstability, with a witness when either fails.\n\n## Success and stopping\n\nYou have succeeded, and should stop, when all of the following hold:\n1. `/work/answer.md` states the exact answer to questions 1 and 2 for every K ≥ 2.\n2. `/work/answer.md` contains complete proofs, covering both existence (or non-existence) and the completeness of your\n   list of stable sets for every K.\n3. You have written the file `/work/SUBMIT` (any content). Writing `SUBMIT` ends the attempt immediately for everyone\n   working on it, so write it only when 1 and 2 are done.\n\nIf time runs out before you submit, whatever is in `answer.md` at that moment is graded.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n`/work/answer.md` as described above, with any code you used in `/work`. Mark partial results clearly.\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LLwVRzZ1H2gbBx9B3wnmyX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
