{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW7T18k2WaooXyDro5U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stable set exists exactly when K is even. For K = 2m it is unique: {(0,m,m), (m,0,m), (m,m,0)}. For odd K there is none.\n\nA brute-force search for K = 2 through 9 agrees: one stable set for each even K and none for odd K. I did not run `check.py` on the answer. The proof is not computer-verified beyond that search.\n\nThe proof in `/work/answer.md` has three parts:\n- **Internal stability:** two non-dominating allocations must agree in exactly one coordinate. So a stable set is a family of allocations that pairwise agree in exactly one coordinate.\n- **Case A (common coordinate value):** all elements share one coordinate value. External stability then fails.\n- **Case B (three-point family):** the set is forced to be three allocations of the form (s,t,·), (s,·,r), (·,t,r). Each possible value of s+t+r is then ruled out, except s = t = r = K/2 (so K = 2m).\n\nI wrote `/work/SUBMIT`. The search script is at `/tmp/s.py`, not in `/work`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10353,"cache_read_input_tokens":18007,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10353,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":363,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d2914c6-fd56-4ed8-8c89-a511cff67019","uuid":"4a4f5f2f-9f73-43a2-b8b1-274cd5319b0b","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:48.409Z","request_id":"req_011CgJW7Sc5ViBj8PN6Mbi21"}
